 "desc_header": "IN THIS LESSON",
 "lyrics": "A zebra's stripes make it hard for biting flies to land: horses in striped coats got far fewer fly landings (Caro et al., University of Bristol / UC Davis, PLOS ONE 2019).\nEach zebra's stripes are unique, like a human fingerprint; foals can stand and walk soon after birth; zebras eat mostly grass (Live Science, 'Zebra Facts').\nPigs' sweat glands do little to cool them, so they wallow in mud; mud also protects their skin from sunburn (Britannica Kids, 'pig'; Bracke 2011, Applied Animal Behaviour Science).\nPigs root for food with their snouts and have a good sense of smell; piglets are born with a well-developed sense of smell (Britannica Kids, 'pig').\nA koala is a marsupial, not a bear; its baby is called a joey and grows in the mother's pouch (San Diego Zoo; Australian Koala Foundation).\nKoalas eat mainly eucalyptus (gum) leaves and sleep 18 to 22 hours a day (Australian Koala Foundation, savethekoala.com).",
 "script": "Hello, friends! I am Milo the bluebird.\nToday we are going to play a guessing game!\nThree animal friends are hiding.\nI will give you three clues. Then you guess who it is.\nPut on your thinking cap. Are you ready? Let's play!\nGuess who! Here are three clues.\nClue one. This friend looks like a horse.\nClue two. This friend has black and white stripes.\nClue three. This friend lives on the sunny, grassy savanna. Who is it?\nIt is the zebra! Great guessing!\nBut why does a zebra wear stripes? What do you think?\nHere is the fun fact. A zebra's stripes help keep the pesky flies away!\nLook. The flies land on the plain brown horse.\nLet's count the flies on the horse. One, two, three, four, five!\nBut on the stripy zebra? The stripes mix the flies up. They cannot land!\nAnd here is one more thing. Every zebra has its own stripes.\nNo two zebras are the same. The stripes are like your fingerprint!\nA baby zebra is called a foal. It can stand up and walk soon after it is born!\nWhat does a zebra eat? Grass, grass, and more grass!\nSay it with me. Stripes keep the flies away!\nCan you gallop like a zebra? Clip, clop, clip, clop!\nQuick check! Do the stripes keep the flies away? Yes, or no?\nYes! Stripes keep the flies away. You are so clever!\nGuess who! Here are three clues.\nClue one. This friend is pink, with a curly tail.\nClue two. This friend lives on the farm.\nClue three. This friend says oink, oink! Who is it?\nIt is the pig! You got it!\nWhat does a pig do on a hot, sunny day? What do you think?\nHere is the fun fact. Pigs roll in the mud to keep cool!\nWhen you are hot, you sweat. But a pig cannot sweat much.\nSo the pig rolls in cool, squishy mud. The mud even stops sunburn, like sunscreen!\nNow look at that nose! A pig's nose is called a snout.\nThe snout is super strong. A pig uses it to dig in the ground for yummy food.\nAnd the snout can smell very, very well. Sniff, sniff, sniff!\nBaby pigs are called piglets. Even a tiny piglet has a super nose!\nA pig loves a mud bath, just like you love a cool bath on a hot day!\nSay it with me. Pigs roll in mud to keep cool!\nCan you roll your arms like a pig in the mud? Roll, roll, roll!\nQuick check! Why does a pig roll in the mud? To keep cool? Or to keep warm?\nTo keep cool! Super thinking!\nGuess who! Here are three clues.\nClue one. This friend is gray and fluffy, with a big black nose.\nClue two. This friend loves to hug a tree.\nClue three. This friend munches on green leaves. Who is it?\nIt is the koala! Well done!\nNow, a tricky question. Is a koala a bear?\nNo! Here is the fun fact. A koala is not a bear!\nLook. This is a bear. A bear has no pouch.\nBut a mama koala has a pouch on her tummy, like a cozy pocket. Her baby rides inside!\nA baby koala is called a joey.\nWhat does a koala eat? Leaves! Leaves from the gum tree. Munch, munch, munch!\nAnd a koala is a super sleeper. It sleeps almost all day long!\nA koala eats leaves, sleeps, eats more leaves, and sleeps again. What a cozy life!\nKoalas live far away, in a country called Australia.\nSay it with me. A koala is not a bear!\nCan you hug yourself, like a koala hugs a tree? Squeeze!\nQuick check! What is a baby koala called? A cub? Or a joey?\nA joey! You remembered!\nWow! You guessed all three friends. Now it is quiz time!\nGet ready. Think hard!\nWhose stripes keep the flies away?\nThe zebra! Yes!\nWho rolls in the mud to keep cool?\nThe pig! That's right!\nWho is not a bear, and rides in a pouch as a baby?\nThe koala! Good job!\nYou learned so many new things today.\nGallop like a zebra. Roll like a pig. And hug like a koala!\nWhich friend was the hardest to guess?\nThank you for playing with me. Bye-bye, friends! See you next time!",
